1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,137
Wavelength is why some antennas are the size of a person and others hide inside a

2
00:00:07,137 --> 00:00:07,835
laptop lid.

3
00:00:07,835 --> 00:00:12,909
It's the physical distance a wave covers in one complete cycle — the gap between

4
00:00:12,909 --> 00:00:16,842
one crest and the next — written with the Greek letter lambda.

5
00:00:16,842 --> 00:00:22,106
And it's inversely proportional to frequency: higher frequency, shorter wavelength.

6
00:00:22,106 --> 00:00:27,370
If frequency counts cycles in time, wavelength measures one cycle laid out in space

7
00:00:27,370 --> 00:00:30,478
— the spatial twin of frequency's temporal count.

8
00:00:30,928 --> 00:00:32,427
Anchor it with a walk.

9
00:00:32,427 --> 00:00:37,740
Two people move down a street at exactly the same speed — but one takes quick,

10
00:00:37,740 --> 00:00:40,806
short steps and the other slow, long strides.

11
00:00:40,806 --> 00:00:45,506
Steps per second is frequency; the length of each step is wavelength.

12
00:00:45,506 --> 00:00:50,888
Because the travel speed is fixed, more steps per second forces each step to be

13
00:00:50,888 --> 00:00:51,432
shorter.

14
00:00:51,432 --> 00:00:57,087
Radio waves all walk at one speed — the speed of light — so the same bargain holds.

15
00:00:57,537 --> 00:01:02,969
That bargain is one clean formula: lambda equals c over f — wavelength is the speed

16
00:01:02,969 --> 00:01:04,933
of light divided by frequency.

17
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:10,234
Plug in c, about three times ten-to-the-eighth metres per second, and you get the

18
00:01:10,234 --> 00:01:15,209
shortcut every radio operator memorizes: wavelength in metres is roughly 300

19
00:01:15,209 --> 00:01:17,696
divided by the frequency in megahertz.

20
00:01:17,696 --> 00:01:19,790
One division, done in your head.

21
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:20,756
Try it.

22
00:01:20,756 --> 00:01:25,546
A 150-megahertz VHF signal: 300 over 150 — about two metres long.

23
00:01:25,546 --> 00:01:29,746
A 460-megahertz UHF signal: about sixty-five centimetres.

24
00:01:29,746 --> 00:01:32,252
One gigahertz: thirty centimetres.

25
00:01:32,252 --> 00:01:38,295
And Wi-Fi at 2.4 gigahertz is down around twelve and a half centimetres — which is

26
00:01:38,295 --> 00:01:44,116
exactly why its antenna fits inside a laptop lid while a two-metre-band antenna

27
00:01:44,116 --> 00:01:46,180
stands roughly a metre tall.

28
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:52,296
Zoom out and the range is staggering: as you climb the spectrum, wavelengths shrink

29
00:01:52,296 --> 00:01:58,413
from kilometres at the low-frequency end to millimetres at the top of the microwave

30
00:01:58,413 --> 00:02:02,024
range — the origin of the term "millimetre wave."

31
00:02:02,474 --> 00:02:04,118
Why does the antenna care?

32
00:02:04,118 --> 00:02:09,178
Because an efficient antenna is resonant when its length is a simple fraction of

33
00:02:09,178 --> 00:02:14,049
the wavelength: a half-wave dipole is lambda over two, a quarter-wave whip is

34
00:02:14,049 --> 00:02:16,389
lambda over four over a ground plane.

35
00:02:16,389 --> 00:02:21,639
Get the fraction wrong and the antenna presents a poor impedance match — reflecting

36
00:02:21,639 --> 00:02:24,358
power instead of radiating or capturing it.

37
00:02:24,358 --> 00:02:29,165
Wavelength also sets how waves negotiate the world: they bend readily around

38
00:02:29,165 --> 00:02:34,415
objects small compared to lambda, but cast sharp shadows behind objects much larger

39
00:02:34,415 --> 00:02:34,921
than it.

40
00:02:34,921 --> 00:02:40,045
Long-wavelength HF hugs terrain and refracts off the ionosphere; short-wavelength

41
00:02:40,045 --> 00:02:44,535
UHF behaves more like light — line-of-sight, with pronounced multipath.

42
00:02:44,985 --> 00:02:49,888
Here's a field skill hiding in this: the size of a repeater's antenna or a radar

43
00:02:49,888 --> 00:02:54,729
dish is a quick visual estimate of its operating wavelength — and therefore its

44
00:02:54,729 --> 00:02:55,036
band.

45
00:02:55,036 --> 00:02:57,794
Hardware wears its wavelength on the outside.

46
00:02:58,244 --> 00:03:03,288
Wavelength never appears in GopherTrunk's sample stream — the software works in

47
00:03:03,288 --> 00:03:07,949
frequency and time — but it governs everything in front of the converter.

48
00:03:07,949 --> 00:03:12,993
The VHF and UHF land-mobile bands GopherTrunk targets run from about two metres

49
00:03:12,993 --> 00:03:18,101
down to thirty centimetres of wavelength, which is why discone and vertical whip

50
00:03:18,101 --> 00:03:20,910
antennas of that scale are the usual choice.

51
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:26,018
Pick an antenna mismatched to your target and the signal meter in dBFS tells the

52
00:03:26,018 --> 00:03:29,785
story: a starved receiver that no amount of DSP can rescue.

53
00:03:30,235 --> 00:03:35,306
So: wavelength is one cycle measured in metres, it's 300 over the frequency in

54
00:03:35,306 --> 00:03:40,702
megahertz, and it's the number that physically sizes every antenna you'll ever use.

55
00:03:40,702 --> 00:03:42,457
Full write-up linked below.

